#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b75a7f1-0e86-5f90-a486-c59451c77760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151399} {:answer/id #uuid "c9007251-dbb6-5962-844c-197e86552c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151400} {:answer/id #uuid "eeadfbb6-9cc4-58fc-a0a7-8c385fee8584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151401} {:answer/id #uuid "27dff005-3cf9-5db7-b4d5-e7d701eec09d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151402} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2393f5-3e9f-59f1-9ec2-3b9a3d4c3f1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeadfbb6-9cc4-58fc-a0a7-8c385fee8584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151401} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f28f148-5a63-5031-99db-0b9ca13b7095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8098b383-f4a9-5d0e-9352-b359bad47678" :question/math-academy-id "q-80430" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/d7db51d6af763e8853f815feb6e74b88d0ca9a19e79c42b3a42da4e8b088f93c.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The displayed region has points above $y=1$, so rectangle I, whose vertical range is $[-1,1]$, does not contain all of $D$. The region also has points with $x<0$, so rectangle II, whose horizontal range is $[0,2]$, does not contain all of $D$. The entire drawn region lies in rectangle III, $[-2,2]\\times[-2,2]$.\n\nFor rectangle III, $f=x+y$ on $D$ and is zero on $R\\setminus D$. Therefore $\\iint_R f\\,dA=\\iint_D(x+y)\\,dA$. Only III meets the explicit containment requirement, giving \"III only\"."}]}
